Here is a nutrient-dense meal plan designed to hit approximately 3,500 – 3,750 calories. Since you are "critical" of what you eat, this focuses on whole foods, healthy fats, and high-quality protein to support your 30km cycling and daily exercises without using "junk" calories.
Clean Bulk Meal Plan (~3,750 kcal)
1. Breakfast (The Cycling Fuel) – ~900 kcal
Oatmeal: 1 cup (approx. 100-120g) of dry oats cooked with 1.5 cups of whole milk (or fortified plant milk).
Boost: 2 tablespoons of natural Peanut or Almond Butter stirred in.
Fruit: 1 large Banana (sliced).
Protein: 3 Eggs (scrambled or boiled) on the side.
2. Mid-Morning Snack – ~400 kcal
Trail Mix: 50g of raw Walnuts or Almonds.
Yogurt: 1 cup of plain Greek Yogurt (full fat, no added sugar) with a handful of berries.
3. Lunch (Recovery & Energy) – ~850 kcal
Carbs: 1.5 cups (cooked weight) of Brown Rice, Quinoa, or Whole Wheat Pasta.
Protein: 150g of Grilled Chicken Breast, Turkey, or Salmon.
Healthy Fat: 1/2 of an Avocado.
Vegetables: Large portion of Broccoli or Spinach drizzled with 1 tablespoon of Extra Virgin Olive Oil.
4. Pre-Ride / Afternoon Snack – ~450 kcal
Smoothie: 1 scoop of Protein Powder, 1 tablespoon of Chia seeds, 1 cup of milk, and 1/2 cup of oats blended together.
Quick Fuel: 1 Apple or a few dates for natural glucose before your 30km ride.
5. Dinner (Muscle Repair) – ~850 kcal
Carbs: 2 large Sweet Potatoes (roasted).
Protein: 180g of Lean Beef, Steak, or Tuna steak.
Vegetables: Mixed roasted vegetables (peppers, zucchini, onions) drizzled generously with 1.5 tablespoons of Extra Virgin Olive Oil.
6. Late Night Snack (Optional) – ~300 kcal
Casein Source: 1 cup of Cottage Cheese or Quark (slow-digesting protein for sleep).
Fat: 10-12 Cashews.
Key Strategy for a "Critical" Eater:
The Olive Oil Trick: Since you don't like sugar, use Extra Virgin Olive Oil. Adding just 2 tablespoons across your day adds 240 clean calories without making you feel "stuffed."
Hydration is Vital: Eating this much food and protein requires your kidneys to work harder. Aim for 3.5 to 4 liters of water daily, especially with your cycling.
Consistency: Because your metabolism is so high at 183cm, missing just one day of these meals will stall your growth. You have to treat eating like your training.
